# The keys allowed to sign an OpenMouse release tag (docs/RELEASING.md, "Signed
# release tags").
#
# Format: OpenSSH allowed_signers, one key per line:
#
#   <principal> namespaces="git" <keytype> <key>
#
# scripts/verify-release-tag.sh checks a vX.Y.Z tag against this file alone
# (`git verify-tag` with gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile pointing here), so a key in
# the owner's own git configuration does not count, and scripts/publish-release.ps1
# refuses to build a release from a tag that does not verify. The in-app updater
# does not read this file: Android itself refuses an APK whose signing
# certificate differs from the installed app's, and the updater checks that
# before handing the file to the installer. With no key here, no release tag can
# verify.
#
# One-time setup, done once by the owner of the repository:
#
#   ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/openmouse-release -C openmouse-release
#   git config gpg.format ssh
#   git config user.signingkey ~/.ssh/openmouse-release.pub
#
# then add the public key as a line here, in the form above, with the key type
# and key (the second and third fields of the .pub file), and commit it. A fork
# keeps its own key and its own copy of this file. Lines starting with # are
# comments.
#
# The two keys below are the owner's existing release keys, shared with the
# Polaris repository (its release-signers file): the ed25519 release key, and the
# RSA key of the OK Studio Inc. EV code-signing certificate on the SafeNet eToken
# (`ssh-keygen -i -m PKCS8` of its public key). The verify script here checks SSH
# signatures only; a tag signed with the certificate (X.509) needs the Polaris
# verifier.
polaris-release namespaces="git" ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAILqqDqiDvDnDm6Cf2PgA9bw/YNR7UNACBhbPc0A9My4t
ok-studio-ev namespaces="git" ssh-rsa 
